---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Aperture from "../components/Aperture.astro";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
const notes = (await getCollection("notes")).sort((a, b) => b.data.pubDate.valueOf() - a.data.pubDate.valueOf());
const featured = notes[0];
const amc = "https://github.com/avs-io/agent-memory-contracts";
---
<Base title="Waking Matter" description="Building systems for agents that improve with experience. Memory, provenance and evaluation for repeated work in a changing world." canonical="https://wakingmatter.com/" tone="void" current="home">
  <main id="content">
    <div class="hero-band">
      <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <h1 id="hero-title">Agents should improve with experience.</h1>
          <p class="hero-deck">Most agents still meet each task as strangers to their own past. We build the systems that let useful experience carry forward: what to keep, what to trust, and when to start again.</p>
          <div class="hero-actions">
            <a class="hero-cta" href="#work">See the public work <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
            <a class="hero-cta hero-cta--quiet" href="mailto:hello@wakingmatter.com">Bring a recurring problem <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-figure"><Aperture class="hero-aperture" /></div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="band" aria-labelledby="memory-title">
      <div class="wrap split">
        <p class="label">The problem</p>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2 id="memory-title">Memory is not enough.</h2>
          <p>Retaining information is not learning. What matters is whether yesterday’s work makes today’s decision better.</p>
          <p>That means deciding what to retrieve, what to trust, what to test and what to try differently, and knowing when to forget, reset, stop or ask a person. More history can help, do nothing or make the next decision worse. We hold every system to that test.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" aria-labelledby="questions-label">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="band-head"><p class="label" id="questions-label">Questions we are working on</p></div>
        <div class="trait-grid question-grid">
          <article class="trait"><span class="meta num">01</span><h2>Experience into action</h2><p>How should prior experience change the next allocation of retrieval, attention, compute and action?</p></article>
          <article class="trait"><span class="meta num">02</span><h2>Memory into useful state</h2><p>What should persist, in what form, and for how long? How can its source and limits remain visible?</p></article>
          <article class="trait"><span class="meta num">03</span><h2>Changing worlds</h2><p>How should a system notice that inherited knowledge is stale or harmful, and decide what to revise or reset?</p></article>
          <article class="trait"><span class="meta num">04</span><h2>Evaluation into improvement</h2><p>Does experience improve the outcome, or did we just add context, compute or advice the system already had?</p></article>
        </div>
        <a class="text-link" href="/research/">Explore the research questions <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="work" class="band band--void" aria-labelledby="work-title">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="split">
          <p class="label">Public technical work</p>
          <div class="section-copy">
            <h2 id="work-title">Nothing becomes memory without a decision.</h2>
            <p><a href={amc}>Agent Memory Contracts</a> is our open-source Python library for the boundary between what an agent extracted and what it is allowed to trust.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <ol class="pipeline" aria-label="How a claim becomes trusted memory">
          <li><h3>Source evidence</h3><p>A span in a real source record.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Candidate claim</h3><p>The agent’s interpretation, linked to the evidence it came from.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Reducer decision</h3><p>Provenance, confidence and recency are checked. Failures are rejected with a reason.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Trusted memory</h3><p>A ledger entry that only validates if a decision explicitly authorised it.</p></li>
        </ol>

        <div class="split">
          <p class="label">Reference run</p>
          <div>
            <figure class="transcript">
              <pre><code><span class="t-dim">$ PYTHONPATH=src python examples/reference_reducer.py</span>
=== Scenario A: happy path ===
  candidates:        3
  promoted:          3 ledger entries, 3 reducer decisions
  bundle validated:  OK

=== Scenario B: rejection ===
  rejected:          2
    - reason='no_evidence' (candidate has no evidence_span_ids)
    - reason='low_confidence' (confidence 'low' is below threshold 'medium')
  promoted:          1 ledger entries, 1 reducer decisions

=== Scenario C: validator enforcement ===
  deliberate failure injected: reducer.target_ledger_entry_ids
    does not include the ledger entry that points back at it.
  <span class="t-key">validate_ledger_bundle raised: ValueError(ledger entry is not targeted by reducer decision)</span></code></pre>
              <figcaption class="meta">Abridged output of the <a href={`${amc}/blob/main/examples/reference_reducer.py`}>reference reducer example</a> at revision 6b2b4e5.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <div class="evidence-copy">
              <p>Trustworthy memory is the floor. The next step is showing agents make better decisions because of what they have already done.</p>
              <a class="text-link" href={amc}>Inspect the code and tests <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" aria-labelledby="workflows-title">
      <div class="wrap split">
        <p class="label">Repeated work</p>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2 id="workflows-title">Real work is the test.</h2>
          <p>We want bounded, recurring workflows where agents rediscover context, repeat mistakes or break when facts change. Outcomes have to be checkable, uncertainty visible and escalation to a person explicit.</p>
          <p>The goal is repeated work that gets measurably better without agents getting more confidently wrong. If that is your problem, we want it.</p>
          <a class="text-link" href="mailto:hello@wakingmatter.com">Bring a recurring problem <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band band--void closing" aria-labelledby="thesis-title">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="label">The broader thesis</p>
        <h2 id="thesis-title">Intelligence should accumulate.</h2>
        <div class="closing-copy">
          <p>Useful learning should survive the session and compound across work, agents, models and time. Keeping it useful, revisable and worth inheriting is the work of cumulative intelligence.</p>
          {featured && <a class="text-link" href={`/notes/${featured.slug}/`}>Read the essay: {featured.data.title} <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
